  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Mario A. Posillico was an American construction executive who helped transform his father's Long Island contracting firm into a major infrastructure business. Working with his brothers and Charles Gargano, he expanded its public-works operations and became chairman. His career combined family-company ownership with the construction of roads, bridges and sewer systems across the region.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 91,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Official company history, family notice and federal ownership record support the complementary industrial identity card."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 54,
    "html": "<p>Mario A. Posillico was an American construction executive who helped transform his father's Long Island contracting firm into a major infrastructure business. Working with his brothers and Charles Gargano, he expanded its public-works operations and became chairman. His career combined family-company ownership with the construction of roads, bridges and sewer systems across the region.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Before returning to Long Island's construction industry, Mario A. Posillico studied meteorology with the US Navy and flew aboard typhoon-hunting aircraft from Guam. His task was to gather weather data. The posting also gave him opportunities to compete as a professional golfer in tournaments in Japan and the Philippines.\n\nGolf remained a substantial part of his life. He had captained Lehigh University's team while studying industrial engineering and later won club championships at Huntington Crescent and Indian Hills. In 1993, he won the Metropolitan Golf Association's Senior Amateur Championship at Cherry Valley, adding a regional title to a competitive career stretching back to his student years.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 92,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Family notice gives weather service and sustained golf career; association's official winners list independently confirms1993title and venue."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S4"
    ],
    "word_count": 106,
    "html": "<p>Before returning to Long Island's construction industry, Mario A. Posillico studied meteorology with the US Navy and flew aboard typhoon-hunting aircraft from Guam. His task was to gather weather data. The posting also gave him opportunities to compete as a professional golfer in tournaments in Japan and the Philippines.</p>\r\n<p>Golf remained a substantial part of his life. He had captained Lehigh University's team while studying industrial engineering and later won club championships at Huntington Crescent and Indian Hills. In 1993, he won the Metropolitan Golf Association's Senior Amateur Championship at Cherry Valley, adding a regional title to a competitive career stretching back to his student years.</p>\r\n"
  },
